/T (The CA-3 intervention is a deliberately constructed scheduling-policy defect. The task list contains unequal-cost work, with the first 10% of tasks assigned a higher computational cost. A contiguous block distributor assigns the first block to worker 0, concentrating most expensive tasks on that worker. Other workers can finish early while worker 0 continues, producing load imbalance and a longer overall completion time.)
/E (The CA-3 intervention is a deliberately constructed scheduling-policy defect. The task list contains unequal-cost work, with the first 10% of tasks assigned a higher computational cost. A contiguous block distributor assigns the first block to worker 0, concentrating most expensive tasks on that worker. Other workers can finish early while worker 0 continues, producing load imbalance and a longer overall completion time.)
