486 0 obj
<<
/Title (The CA-3 intervention is a deliberately constructed scheduling-policy defect. The task list contains unequal-cost work, with the first 10% of tasks assigned a higher computational cost. A contiguous block distributor assigns the first block to worker 0, concentrating most expensive tasks on that worker. Other workers can finish early while worker 0 continues, producing load imbalance and a longer overall completion time.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 433 0 R
/Prev 474 0 R
/Next 475 0 R
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Title (how work is assigned to them \(block, round-robin or dynamic distribution\). For CA-3, the failure is framed as an intentionally defective user-space task scheduler: it divides a cost-skewed task list into contiguous equal-count blocks. Equal task counts do not imply equal work. Because the first 10% of tasks are ten times more expensive, a contiguous partition places a disproportionate amount of work in the first worker's block. The other workers may finish sooner and remain idle while the slowest worker determines total completion time.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 465 0 R
/Prev 476 0 R
/Next 488 0 R
>>
endobj
